WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:07.580
On January 23rd, 2014, a mystery emerges that
will take investigators from the deserts of

00:00:07.580 --> 00:00:12.550
Mars. To the birth of one of the greatest
civilizations on earth.

00:00:15.580 --> 00:00:16.960
There's an obvious.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:22.090
Crater in the landscape here in Mexico, but
it's not so much the crater that catches my

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:25.650
attention. It's what's inside this crater.

00:00:27.200 --> 00:00:31.130
This is intriguing because what we're seeing
is this white color.

00:00:31.130 --> 00:00:34.160
And boy, it sure does look like ice.

00:00:35.710 --> 00:00:41.770
It's so unusual because we're talking about
Mexico and this white patch stays here all

00:00:41.770 --> 00:00:42.550
year round.

00:00:47.570 --> 00:00:54.740
The mystery White Crater is one of a cluster
known as the seven luminaries, which, when

00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:57.950
viewed from above, mirrored the Ursa major
constellation.

00:01:03.220 --> 00:01:09.940
To NASA's Kasha Patel, this extraterrestrial
connection may be more than just coincidence.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:14.220
This crater reminds me of something.

00:01:14.220 --> 00:01:15.900
But it's not on Earth.

00:01:15.930 --> 00:01:20.100
This crater looks pretty similar to the
Korolev crater on Mars.

00:01:25.700 --> 00:01:32.750
The Korolev crater in the northern lowlands
of Mars contains enough water ice to fill

00:01:32.750 --> 00:01:35.660
almost a billion Olympic swimming pools.

00:01:36.350 --> 00:01:41.630
Most of the ice found elsewhere on Mars is
mixed with frozen carbon dioxide.

00:01:41.630 --> 00:01:48.620
But it appears that the icy regions in the
Korolev crater are composed of more pure

00:01:48.650 --> 00:01:49.220
water.

00:01:53.890 --> 00:02:01.840
The crater is named after visionary rocket
scientist Sergei Korolev, who during the Cold

00:02:01.840 --> 00:02:08.020
War, helped the Soviets to outsmart NASA by
landing the first spacecraft on the moon.

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:16.540
In a twist of fate, the space agency hopes
his legacy will be key to the future human

00:02:16.540 --> 00:02:18.100
colonization of Mars.

00:02:19.870 --> 00:02:22.900
Ice is such a precious commodity for humans.

00:02:23.020 --> 00:02:25.660
It can also be used as rocket fuel.

00:02:26.470 --> 00:02:29.170
If you can make hydrogen and oxygen on Mars.

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:33.970
Fill your rocket for a one way trip and then
you gas up on the other end and you fly back

00:02:33.970 --> 00:02:34.660
to the earth.

00:02:37.940 --> 00:02:44.450
What stops the ice from melting during Mars's
70 degree Fahrenheit daytime temperatures is

00:02:44.450 --> 00:02:46.730
a process called cold trapping.

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:49.060
Cold air goes.

00:02:49.060 --> 00:02:53.590
Into the crater and it forms sort of an
insulating layer.

00:02:53.590 --> 00:02:56.980
So any of the water gets trapped there as
water ice.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:01.870
Could we be seeing the same process in
action in this image?

00:03:06.000 --> 00:03:13.740
But our planet's atmosphere is 100 times
thicker than on Mars, creating swirling winds

00:03:13.740 --> 00:03:17.640
which prevent cold trapping from taking
place here in Mexico.

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:26.170
The Earth has a very dynamic atmosphere that
tends to even out the environment.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:30.800
So this is something very different that
we're looking at.

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.710
According to those who live nearby.

00:03:37.740 --> 00:03:43.800
The crater did once contain water, but it
was bleach like and killed everything it

00:03:43.800 --> 00:03:48.180
touched. To geologists.

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:53.370
This suggests the mystery white substance is
the legacy of a catastrophically powerful

00:03:53.370 --> 00:03:54.690
volcanic event.

00:03:56.190 --> 00:04:01.230
This means the water inside this crater is
extremely alkaline.

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:05.130
That tells me that this is most likely
sodium bicarbonate.

00:04:05.460 --> 00:04:08.310
It's very broad, but it's very shallow.

00:04:08.340 --> 00:04:12.510
This crater has all the classic features of
a maar volcano.

00:04:17.410 --> 00:04:22.930
Mar volcanoes are formed by phreatic
eruptions, which are known as the nuclear

00:04:22.930 --> 00:04:24.700
bombs of the natural world.

00:04:27.830 --> 00:04:33.440
The more volcano occurs when shallow magma
intrudes into an area that has a lot of

00:04:33.440 --> 00:04:37.800
groundwater. It creates an amazing amount of
energy.

00:04:40.580 --> 00:04:43.560
Vaporizes this water, and it creates a steam
explosion.

00:04:48.660 --> 00:04:52.740
After the explosion, water fills into the
bottom of the crater.

00:04:53.370 --> 00:04:58.800
Sometimes you can get volcanic gases still
seeping in, so you end up with this highly

00:04:58.830 --> 00:05:01.590
alkaline volcanic soup.

00:05:02.730 --> 00:05:09.630
Over time, the ground around the crater dries
out, concentrating the volcanic soup into a

00:05:09.630 --> 00:05:10.950
chemical sludge.

00:05:13.680 --> 00:05:19.590
As that water evaporates, it concentrates it
further until we get these large fields of

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:22.740
crystals that form, which is what we see in
the image.

00:05:25.190 --> 00:05:29.780
Scientists have recently discovered the
crater's floor is covered with a

00:05:29.780 --> 00:05:32.690
cyanobacteria called spirulina.

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:39.700
Spirulina itself is a very simple organism,
but over history it's been considered a

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:40.600
superfood.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:47.060
What we found is that the Aztecs used it as
one of their primary food sources.

00:05:48.050 --> 00:05:50.100
They'd harvest large quantities of it.

00:05:50.120 --> 00:05:52.730
They considered it the food of the gods.

00:05:53.750 --> 00:05:58.490
Around 100,000 years after it devastated this
part of Mexico.

00:05:58.610 --> 00:06:03.170
This strange white crater may have helped
give rise to one of the greatest

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.940
civilizations in history.

00:06:05.390 --> 00:06:11.160
This is almost like a museum of the Earth's
earliest prehistory.

00:06:11.180 --> 00:06:12.560
It's a fascinating place.

00:06:17.430 --> 00:06:20.250
July 18th, 2019.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.520
High above Kansas.

00:06:23.670 --> 00:06:24.810
Eyes in the sky.

00:06:24.840 --> 00:06:29.130
Capture something out of place in a farming
community far below.

00:06:29.850 --> 00:06:32.850
At first glance, it looks normal.

00:06:33.580 --> 00:06:34.990
But if you look closer.

00:06:35.350 --> 00:06:41.440
There's some structures here that a farmer
would not have on their homestead.

00:06:43.870 --> 00:06:49.990
A double security fence surrounds a 105 foot
diameter flat concrete platform.

00:06:51.220 --> 00:06:57.010
And a close analysis of the approach road
reveals This is no ordinary structure.

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.220
Paying attention to the shadows is really
important here.

00:07:01.250 --> 00:07:05.900
Where the road comes in to the compound, the
shadows really give the impression that the

00:07:05.900 --> 00:07:09.230
road doesn't stay flat, but actually dips
down.

00:07:11.280 --> 00:07:17.550
Is this compound hiding secrets that are
somehow related to a more sinister purpose.

00:07:18.810 --> 00:07:24.900
Military analyst Carlos Munoz finds some
historic images that seem to confirm that

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:27.870
this rural landscape conceals a terrifying
secret.

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:32.870
The remote location, the security perimeter.

00:07:32.870 --> 00:07:33.590
What looks.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.110
Like an underground bunker.

00:07:35.120 --> 00:07:38.330
It is the launch site for a nuclear missile.

00:07:40.650 --> 00:07:46.500
Declassified military records confirm this
location was one of a series of top secret

00:07:46.500 --> 00:07:50.460
bunkers. Armed with Atlas E nuclear
missiles.

00:07:52.470 --> 00:07:54.660
The Atlas missiles were the first
intercontinental.

00:07:55.590 --> 00:07:59.940
Ballistic missiles armed with enough
destructive power to destroy a city the size

00:07:59.940 --> 00:08:02.130
of Moscow or Saint Petersburg.

00:08:03.750 --> 00:08:09.420
What puzzles analysts is that the Atlas E was
mothballed in 1964.

00:08:10.860 --> 00:08:13.410
But the missile silo doesn't appear to be
abandoned.

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.500
If you look at the satellite images, you'll
see the road, you see the cars, you see a

00:08:19.500 --> 00:08:23.550
digger. So, you know, there's activity
happening at the site right now.

